d2d1: Do not add a join where a path goes straight on.

d2d_geometry_outline_add_join() treated every ccw == 0 join as a reversal and added a
quad extending 25 units along the incoming direction. ccw is also 0 when two segments
are exactly collinear, which is common in densely sampled polylines, so those got
straight spikes off the curve (Audition Parametric Equalizer curve: a ribbon with
gaps; Windows draws a thin line). Test: wine/tests/d2d/polyline.c with eqcurve.txt.

diff --git a/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c b/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c
index 1bb48e2..b8ef100 100644
--- a/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c
@@ -2961,6 +2961,13 @@ static BOOL d2d_geometry_outline_add_join(struct d2d_geometry *geometry,
     f = &geometry->outline.faces[geometry->outline.face_count];
 
     ccw = d2d_point_ccw(&origin, prev, next);
+    /* prev points back along the incoming segment and next along the outgoing one, so
+     * ccw is also 0 when the path simply goes straight on (prev = -next). No join is
+     * needed then; the extension below is only for a reversal (prev = next). Densely
+     * sampled polylines hit this often, and the 25 unit extension drew straight spikes
+     * off the curve (Audition's Parametric Equalizer). */
+    if (ccw == 0.0f && prev->x * next->x + prev->y * next->y < 0.0f)
+        return TRUE;
     if (ccw == 0.0f)
     {
         d2d_outline_vertex_set(&v[0], p0->x, p0->y, -prev->x, -prev->y, -prev->x, -prev->y);
